# {{ .computed.app_title }}

{{ if eq .inputs.kind "desk" -}}
A Surface desk app: a Go server built on `github.com/hollis-labs/surface` ({{ .computed.surface_ref }}) and a page built on
`@hollis-labs/surface-page` (windows that tile, tab and float, the palette, the agent panel, cards). The app's own work is its
modules: Go in `modules/<id>/`, each view in `src/modules/<id>.tsx`.
{{- else -}}
An app whose interface is built on Surface's registry: the frame and views, from `@surface` components.
{{- end }}

## The interface

Its rules are Surface's. Read `docs/gui.md` in github.com/hollis-labs/surface before you style anything.

- **Anything that draws comes from the registry.** Build views from `@/components/ui/*`, arranged with Tailwind
  utilities. No raw `<button>`, `<input>`, `<select>` or `<textarea>`: lint fails.
- **Colours only as the theme's tokens** (`bg-card`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`). No hex, `rgb()` or `oklch()`
  in a view. `className` on a component says where it sits (`w-64`, `flex-1`, `ml-auto`), never how it looks.
- **Never edit a file under `src/components/`.** Those are installed copies of the registry at the ref in `ui.lock.json`
  ({{ .computed.surface_ref }}). `npx surface-ui verify` fails CI when one differs.
- **A look is changed in the registry or the theme, never in this app.** A different colour, radius or font everywhere, a
  component that looks different, a new variant, a component the registry lacks: all of these are changes in the Surface
  repository (`registry/ui/`, `registry/theme/surface.css`), released with a Surface tag and then synced here.
- **This app looking different from the others** is the one exception: override the theme's variables under `:root` and
  `.dark` in `src/index.css`, nowhere else.
- **Need a component or a variant that is not there?** If the registry has it, install it: `npx surface-ui add <name>` (the
  list is `registry/registry.json` in the Surface repository). If it does not, ask Surface's owner for it, saying what it is
  for and where it is needed; do not build a lookalike here and do not restyle an existing one at the call site.
- **The gallery** ({{ if eq .inputs.kind "desk" }}the Components window, in the apps menu{{ else }}`#/gallery`, source `src/gallery/`{{ end }}) draws the installed components in their variants and
  states. {{ if eq .inputs.kind "desk" }}It comes with `@hollis-labs/surface-page/gallery`.{{ else }}Add a specimen there when you install a component.{{ end }}

## Commands

```sh
make setup        # once: npm install, install the components from the registry{{ if eq .inputs.kind "desk" }}, go mod tidy{{ end }}
make check        # what CI runs: ui-verify, lint, typecheck, build{{ if eq .inputs.kind "desk" }}, go vet, go test, go build{{ end }}
npm run dev       # the page with hot reload{{ if eq .inputs.kind "desk" }}, against a running server (make serve){{ end }}
npx surface-ui add <name>      # install a registry component (and what it needs), then commit it
npx surface-ui verify          # every installed file matches the registry at the lock's ref
npx surface-ui status          # what is installed, from where
```

## Moving to a new Surface release

One version line: the tag `vX.Y.Z` is the registry's ref and the version of every `@hollis-labs/surface-*` package{{ if eq .inputs.kind "desk" }} and of
the Go module{{ end }}. Set `ref` in `ui.lock.json`, the `@hollis-labs/surface-*` versions in `package.json` (exact){{ if eq .inputs.kind "desk" }} and
`github.com/hollis-labs/surface` in `go.mod`{{ end }} to the same release, then `npm install`, `npx surface-ui sync`, `make check`, and
commit.
{{- if eq .inputs.kind "desk" }}

## The server

- A module imports `github.com/hollis-labs/surface/module` and nothing else of Surface. Its view imports only
  `@hollis-labs/surface-page/sdk` and the installed components. `docs/writing-a-module.md` in the Surface repository is the
  guide; `modules/start` is the smallest example.
- Agent tools are Go (`module.Tool`), never page code. The desk is the server's: a view asks the desk, it does not keep
  window state.
- Tests and browser checks use a throwaway `{{ .computed.env_prefix }}_DATA_DIR`.
{{- end }}
